\begin{abstract}
When a large language model (LLM) judge scores a model response on a benchmark item, the result conflates three signals: genuine ability, the judge's scoring tendency, and any advantage from training data exposure. Current evaluation pipelines correct for judge bias and contamination independently, but we show that separate correction is structurally flawed. We formalize this as an omitted variable bias (OVB) problem: any estimator that models one distortion while omitting the other produces systematically biased ability rankings, regardless of correction order. We propose the Gated Many-Facet Rasch Model (G-MFRM), a Bayesian framework that jointly estimates model ability, judge severity, and contamination susceptibility with formal identifiability guarantees and bounded error propagation. In synthetic experiments ($N = 50$), G-MFRM reduces ability root mean squared error by 51\% over sequential pipelines ($p < 0.001$). On Arena-Hard with 9 LLM judges, G-MFRM produces the first joint contamination-severity diagnostic: older models exhibit 4--6$\times$ higher contamination susceptibility, and judge severity profiles span 1.34 logits across the proprietary-open divide. Leave-one-out cross-validation confirms substantially improved predictive fit ($|\Delta\mathrm{ELPD}/\mathrm{SE}| = 5.9$)\footnote{Code and data are available at \url{https://anonymous.4open.science/r/gated_mfrm_judge_bias-43CC/}.}.
\end{abstract}

